WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.480
Every winter, crews drag dredges along the bottom of the Chesapeake Bay.

2
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.260
They sample blue crabs at about 1,500 sites.

3
00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:19.540
From one winter to the next, the estimated number of crabs can change by more than

4
00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:24.100
100 million. So how many crabs can it really hold?

5
00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:31.193
A population is all the members of one species living in the same area at the same

6
00:00:31.193 --> 00:00:35.533
time. The blue crabs in the Bay this winter are a population.

7
00:00:37.013 --> 00:00:41.413
Biotic factors are living parts of the environment, like predators and disease.

8
00:00:42.773 --> 00:00:47.673
Abiotic factors are nonliving parts, like temperature and salinity, the salt

9
00:00:47.673 --> 00:00:52.533
level. A limiting factor keeps a population from growing larger.

10
00:00:53.317 --> 00:00:57.437
Suppose 10 bacteria land in a dish of fresh food.

11
00:00:58.197 --> 00:01:01.477
Each one splits in two every 30 minutes.

12
00:01:02.317 --> 00:01:09.137
Now there are 20, then 40, 80, 160, and 320.

13
00:01:10.317 --> 00:01:12.297
This is exponential growth.

14
00:01:13.077 --> 00:01:15.077
On a graph, it makes a J shape.

15
00:01:16.097 --> 00:01:18.777
No real population can keep this up for long.

16
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.940
Then limits set in.

17
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.060
Food runs short, and crowding makes competition and disease worse.

18
00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:31.480
The curve bends into an S shape and levels off.

19
00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:35.580
The level it settles near is the carrying capacity.

20
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:41.940
Carrying capacity is the largest population an environment can support over time.

21
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:47.560
If few animals move in or out, births and deaths are about equal there.

22
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.200
The numbers still go up and down a little.

23
00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.840
That is dynamic equilibrium.

24
00:01:53.833 --> 00:01:58.313
Carrying capacity is not one number stamped on a species.

25
00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:01.033
It changes when the environment changes.

26
00:02:01.753 --> 00:02:06.293
More food, a mild winter, or more hiding places can raise it.

27
00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:10.613
A hard freeze or the loss of marsh grass can lower it.

28
00:02:11.573 --> 00:02:12.713
People change it too.

29
00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:16.033
Crabbing removes crabs from the Bay each year.

30
00:02:16.817 --> 00:02:19.757
So how many crabs can the Bay hold?

31
00:02:20.577 --> 00:02:23.617
There is a largest number it can support over time.

32
00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:26.877
But that number moves as conditions change.

33
00:02:27.717 --> 00:02:30.797
This is probably why the crab count rises and falls.

34
00:02:31.777 --> 00:02:32.777
Remember this rule.

35
00:02:33.657 --> 00:02:35.597
Carrying capacity is not fixed.

36
00:02:36.237 --> 00:02:37.717
It moves with the environment.
